  <credit page="1">
    <credit-type>title</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="1114.62" default-y="1611.01" justify="right" valign="top" font-size="24">The “St. Louis Blues”</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="1">
    <credit-type>subtitle</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="600.17" default-y="1553.86" justify="center" valign="top">Respectfully Dedicated To Mr. Russell Gardiner.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>“The Banner Buggy Man”</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="1">
    <credit-type>composer</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="1114.62" default-y="1502.75" justify="right" valign="bottom" font-size="11">W. C. Handy</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="2">
    <credit-type>title</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="85.71" default-y="1583.98" justify="left" valign="top" font-size="18">Editorial Notes</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="2">
    <credit-words default-x="85.71" default-y="1533.18" justify="left" valign="top" font-size="8">1. Dedication. The source reads: “Respectfully Dedicated To Mr. Russell Gardiner. ‘The Banner Buggy Man.’” The spelling “Gardiner” is
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>retained as printed. The epithet very likely identifies Russell E. Gardner, listed in 1912 as sole proprietor of the Banner Buggy Co.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Source: Albert Nelson Marquis, ed., The Book of St. Louisans, 2nd ed. (St. Louis: St. Louis Republic, 1912), 214.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>2. m. 1, piano left hand, beat 1: The source prints a shared notehead with opposing stems and a single augmentation dot.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>It is encoded as a dotted eighth in the upper-stem voice, beamed to the following sixteenth, and as a dotted quarter in the lower-stem voice;
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>the printed dot is understood to apply to both durations.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>3. Original edition notice. The source copy—a 1914 Pace &amp; Handy Music Co. issue preserved by the Gaylord Music Library at
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>Washington University in St. Louis—prints:
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>        “Copyright MCMXIV by W. C. Handy. Published by The Pace &amp; Handy Music Co., Memphis, Tenn.”
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>The notice is reproduced as historical source information and does not describe the present legal status of this edition.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>The 1914 score has returned to the public domain in the United States, although it may remain restricted in other jurisdictions.
</credit-words>
    <credit-words>The CC0 dedication applies only to this transcription and its editorial contributions.</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="1">
    <credit-type>rights</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="600.17" default-y="85.71" justify="center" valign="bottom" font-size="9">Transcription and editorial contributions dedicated under CC0 1.0 Universal; third-party rights are unaffected.</credit-words>
    </credit>
  <credit page="2">
    <credit-type>rights</credit-type>
    <credit-words default-x="600.17" default-y="85.71" justify="center" valign="bottom" font-size="9">Transcription and editorial contributions dedicated under CC0 1.0 Universal; third-party rights are unaffected.</credit-words>
    </credit>
